      "strengths_deep_dive": [
        "The ICNA Council for Social Justice demonstrates a high level of financial health and transparency. Their <cite id=\"14\">fundraising efficiency of $0.10 per $1 raised</cite> is excellent, allowing more resources to stay within their core programs. The organization's board is <cite id=\"15\">100% independent</cite>, which ensures strong oversight and governance. Furthermore, their <cite id=\"16\">theory of change is documented</cite>, providing a clear roadmap for how their advocacy leads to social justice outcomes."
      ],
      "summary": "The ICNA Council for Social Justice (CSJ) operates as a dedicated advocacy and direct service organization focused on the rights of the poor and oppressed. In FY2024, the organization managed <cite id=\"1\">$763,582 in total revenue</cite> to fund its mission of human rights protection. The organization maintains a <cite id=\"2\">three-star rating from Charity Navigator</cite> with an <cite id=\"3\">overall score of 86.5/100</cite>, reflecting a solid commitment to accountability. Their work is particularly focused on <cite id=\"4\">Muslim prisoner support</cite>, racial justice, and <cite id=\"5\">immigration justice</cite>. By providing direct legal support and fostering civic action, CSJ addresses systemic issues affecting Muslim communities in the United States. The organization demonstrates high financial efficiency, directing <cite id=\"6\">82.6% of its expenses toward programs</cite> in FY2023. This ratio significantly <cite id=\"7\">exceeds the 75% industry standard</cite> for non-profits. While revenue saw a <cite id=\"8\">decline of 8% in the most recent fiscal year</cite>, the organization remains resilient with <cite id=\"9\">7.3 months of working capital</cite> available. This financial cushion allows for continued operations despite fluctuations in annual contributions. The organization is <cite id=\"10\">verified as zakat-eligible</cite>, specifically serving categories such as the poor (fuqara) and the needy (masakin). Through its <cite id=\"11\">Muslim Prisoner Support Project</cite>, CSJ provides essential religious materials and advocacy for incarcerated individuals. Their <cite id=\"12\">civic engagement initiatives</cite> empower community members to participate in the democratic process and fight Islamophobia.",
